lesson.text:
Hard research passages contain precise details about study design. Statements may alter one of them:

who was studied (patients with one condition, not everyone);

how many people took part;

how long the study lasted;

what the comparison group received.

An altered detail that the passage specifies differently → FALSE. A detail the passage never mentions → NOT GIVEN. Also watch for claims that turn "reported feeling better" into "was cured", which changes the kind of result.

A writer may support something only under certain conditions: "Offsets can be useful if they are verified", "provided that...", "as long as...", "only when...". Statements may: drop the condition ("offsets are useful") — usually NO if the writer stresses the condition, because the statement overgeneralises;

keep the condition — YES;

introduce a different condition — often NOT GIVEN.

Many Medium passages take a "yes, but" position: the idea is valuable, but it has been exaggerated or misapplied. Such writers produce statements of three kinds:

the core idea the writer accepts → YES;

the exaggerated version the writer rejects → NO;

details the writer doesn't evaluate → NOT GIVEN.

Keep two lists as you read: what the writer accepts and what the writer rejects.

Opinion passages often list criticisms of a policy and respond to each one. The writer's response may:

reject the criticism ("this argument does not hold"), so a statement repeating the criticism → NO;

accept it ("this is a genuine concern") → YES;

partly accept it ("there is something in this, but..."). Here you must work out which part the writer accepts.

Label each criticism in the margin with ✔, ✘ or ½.

Hard writers rarely simply support or oppose. They may:

endorse some uses (→ YES for those uses);

reject a strong claim made by enthusiasts (→ NO);

raise a concern without resolving it (→ statements claiming the writer has resolved it are NO; statements about other, unaddressed aspects are NOT GIVEN).

Watch for sentences that combine these moves: "Nudges have their place, though not the place their champions claim."
